From b0077f542fdf4839a033f498969dc8452004a64f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ebg-ant <296364998+ebg-ant@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 13:03:48 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 3/5] fix: rotate takes itself back on paper: nobody flies
 there and back for "not enough room"

`rotate` cannot know how small the apps let their windows get. It turned the group and laid out; a window refused its new slot and was a
tile; the command took itself back and laid out again: ten moves on an ordinary desk, and nothing had changed.

The layout asks each window where it stands, before anybody moves. At a refusal `rotate` judges again, on paper, and takes itself
back there (`thinkingAgain`). Nobody changes place. What is left: a window that was asked before the one that refused has been
narrower or lower for a moment, where it stood.

Its script leaves `ChaosKnownFailures.swift` and lives on in `nobodyChangesPlaceForARefusal`. The entry stays, for the rule still breaks. "A
docked window becomes a tile, and a moment later the view follows the keyboard onto it" keeps its sentence, over a script from a walk in
which no neighbour can pay: the fault is alive. The script that stood there has been silent since the window with the keyboard gets
its room at a neighbour's cost (played to its end: no rule, and not masked: it never becomes a tile), and a list of what fails holds
what fails: it goes, by the product owner's word. And `desktop`, after which the view follows the keyboard back to the window that
has just left.
